Linux kernel
============

Datiscum Linux 6.18.40 branch
-----------------------------

The linux-6.18.40-datiscum branch adds the scheduler accounting fix used
to prevent the observed scheduler failure on heavily parallel many-core
systems.

The original failure pattern included:

a scheduler divide-by-zero panic in the CFS/EEVDF accounting path,
specifically around avg_vruntime() / scheduler weight accounting
soft lockups
hard lockups
RCU stalls / RCU-preempt starvation
task starvation
blocked or stalled kernel workers under load
intermittent VM hangs under KVM workloads

The affected production system has two AMD EPYC 7643 48-Core Processors
(96 physical cores in total). The patched kernel is currently running
stably on that system after the scheduler failures previously observed
there.

The branch also carries the selected Intel Xe stability backports, including
the Battlemage D3Cold related change, plus the other local kernel updates.

For KVM / VFIO use with the Intel Xe device, the PCI runtime power policy must
also be forced to on on this system. Leaving the device in automatic
runtime power management can still lead to the VM hanging later, even with the
kernel-side Xe fixes applied.

Example for the affected Xe device:

modprobe xe probe_display=0
cat /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:04:00.0/power/control
echo on > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:04:00.0/power/control
cat /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:04:00.0/power/control

Expected final state:

on

This keeps the PCI device powered instead of allowing automatic runtime power
transitions while it is used for KVM passthrough.

See README.datiscum for the exact scheduler guard, Xe commit list,
configuration, scope, runtime power note, and validation details.

There are several guides for kernel developers and users. These guides can
be rendered in a number of formats, like HTML and PDF. Please read
Documentation/admin-guide/README.rst first.

In order to build the documentation, use make htmldocs or
make pdfdocs. The formatted documentation can also be read online at:

https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/

There are various text files in the Documentation/ subdirectory, several
of them using the reStructuredText markup notation.

Please read the Documentation/process/changes.rst file, as it contains
the requirements for building and running the kernel, and information about
the problems which may result from upgrading your kernel.
 
 
